  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1216 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThere is Slight (level 2 of 4) ERO area over Val Verde county for\nThursday night into early Friday, and given the relatively low \nimpact area and antecedent dryness, this is probably appropriate \nwith maybe a 1-3, isolated 4 plus type of rain potential over \nwestern parts of the county. Sadly, a TUTT low that moved over the\nYucatan peninsula into the southern Gulf brought a wave of dry \nmid level air out from the mid/upper ridge center and into Central\nTX. This change lowered our hopes of getting a productive frontal\nrain as was depicted in the model data from several days ago. The\nbest we can hope for over the I-35 corridor is maybe 1 to 2 \ntenths from some showers as the front leaks into the area weakly \nThursday night and tries to deepen around 12Z Friday. PoPs were \nnudge upward ever slightly over the NBM for the early to late \nmorning hours Friday, but most areas should figure on getting a \nbrief sprinkle for a trace or a couple hundredths. \n\nRapid cooling happens as the front deepens daybreak Friday, and \nthe model trends are pointing down for the Friday maxes, \nespecially over the northern counties. Winds driving this cold air\nsurge are expected to approach, but remain just shy of wind \nadvisory levels. As this front looks to be a bit stronger than the\nlast one, and based on some raw data such as the RRFS, we could \nsee the temps struggle to get to much above the low 60s over the \nnorthern Hill Country, though our forecast mixes in the slightly \nwarmer NBM for now. Cloud cover will probably carry into Saturday \nwhich could limit the overnight cooling some and hinder the \nfollowing afternoon max temp. We think that skies should clear up \nmostly for Saturday night, and this could mean a cooling trend may\nnot be emphasized enough for the Sunday morning low. Another \npleasantly cool day continues Sunday before the winds begin to \nturn and start back up on the warming trend for Monday. \n\nA vague zonal pattern early next week leaves this forecaster in \nlow confidence on what happens in terms of warming, upper ridge \nbuilding and the tropical connection to cyclones over the Pacific.\nAs it appears for now we should see a potential for light \noverrunning to reinforce mild temps and daily rain chances to \nfinish out the 7-day forecast.",
